WEBVTT

00:00:00.102 --> 00:00:05.822
Boolean columns can also contain null Suppose
consent to contact

00:00:05.848 --> 00:00:10.617
is a Boolean field TRUE can mean the person
explicitly said yes

00:00:11.532 --> 00:00:16.081
FALSE can mean the person explicitly said no But
null may mean

00:00:16.107 --> 00:00:20.540
that the answer was never recorded Those three
states can have different

00:00:20.566 --> 00:00:26.404
business meanings PostgreSQL lets us test them
explicitly using IS TRUE IS

00:00:26.429 --> 00:00:31.828
FALSE and IS UNKNOWN Run the example in the browser
and look

00:00:31.854 --> 00:00:36.301
at the three output columns They clearly show which
state each record

00:00:36.314 --> 00:00:43.154
belongs to You can copy the query into pgAdmin as
well The

00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:46.453
main lesson is not to automatically treat FALSE and
UNKNOWN as the

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:50.836
same thing In many applications no and we do not
know should

00:00:50.862 --> 00:00:52.112
lead to different actions
